<div class="editorial-layout"><div class="chapter-list"><details class="plan-chapter" id="project-detail-1" open><summary><span class="chapter-count">01</span><h3>The plan for your space</h3>
<span aria-hidden="true" class="chapter-toggle">+</span></summary><div class="chapter-body prose"><p>Broomfield presents a specific challenge a lot of landscapers overlook. It’s not just about picking drought-tolerant plants, it’s about picking ones your HOA will actually approve, and doing it in a way that satisfies the city’s own published guidance on landscape strips. Get either piece wrong and you end up redoing work that should have been right the first time.</p>

<p>Ridgewell Landscape and Design has spent over a decade designing landscapes across the Denver metro, and Broomfield’s particular combination of city guidelines and HOA oversight is something we plan around from the very first conversation.</p>
</div>
</details>
<details class="plan-chapter" id="project-detail-2"><summary><span class="chapter-count">02</span><h3>What Makes a Xeriscape Design “HOA-Friendly”?</h3>
<span aria-hidden="true" class="chapter-toggle">+</span></summary><div class="chapter-body prose"><p>An HOA-friendly xeriscape follows the same water-saving principles as any other drought-tolerant design, native and adapted plants, efficient irrigation, thoughtful layout, but it’s built with your community’s specific covenants in mind from the start. That usually means staying within approved plant lists where they exist, keeping mulch and edging tidy and intentional-looking, and submitting the right documentation before work begins rather than after.</p>
</div>
</details>
<details class="plan-chapter" id="project-detail-3"><summary><span class="chapter-count">03</span><h3>Broomfield’s Own Rules Actually Help Homeowners Here</h3>
<span aria-hidden="true" class="chapter-toggle">+</span></summary><div class="chapter-body prose"><p>The City of Broomfield publishes its own Xeriscaping Guide specifically for landscape strips, the narrow section of yard between the sidewalk and the street that a lot of cities leave homeowners to figure out on their own. Broomfield also partners with Resource Central to support residential lawn replacement, and runs a separate turf replacement rebate program aimed at HOAs and larger properties converting nonfunctional turf.</p>

<p>More importantly for a lot of Broomfield residents, Colorado state law now requires HOAs to permit at least a few pre-approved water-wise garden designs in front yards. That means a community that might have flatly rejected a xeriscape request a few years ago generally can’t anymore, provided the design falls within reasonable, approved parameters.</p>
</div>
</details>
<details class="plan-chapter" id="project-detail-4"><summary><span class="chapter-count">04</span><h3>Our HOA-Friendly Xeriscaping Services in Broomfield</h3>
<span aria-hidden="true" class="chapter-toggle">+</span></summary><div class="chapter-body prose"><h3><strong>HOA Documentation and Design Review</strong></h3>

<p>We design with your specific HOA’s covenants in mind and can help prepare the documentation most architectural review committees expect to see before approving a project.</p>

<h3><strong>Landscape Strip Design</strong></h3>

<p>Since Broomfield has its own published guidance on landscape strips, we design these narrow spaces specifically to meet that guidance, rather than treating them as an afterthought.</p>

<h3><strong>Xeriscape Conversion and Installation</strong></h3>

<p>For the rest of your yard, we handle full lawn removal, soil work, drip irrigation, and planting, all led personally by co-founder Francisco Garcia and lead designer Chris.</p>

<h3><strong>Maintenance</strong></h3>

<p>New plantings need consistent care during their first year or two. We provide maintenance service to keep your landscape looking the way your HOA approved it to look.</p>
</div>
</details>
<details class="plan-chapter" id="project-detail-5"><summary><span class="chapter-count">05</span><h3>Is HOA-Friendly Xeriscaping Right for Your Broomfield Property?</h3>
<span aria-hidden="true" class="chapter-toggle">+</span></summary><div class="chapter-body prose"><ul>
<li>Your HOA has rejected or hesitated on landscaping changes in the past</li>
<li>You live in a planned community like Anthem, where architectural guidelines are detailed and specific</li>
<li>Your landscape strip along the sidewalk is bare, patchy, or hard to maintain</li>
<li>You’re on your HOA’s board and want a design partner who understands the approval process</li>
<li>You want documentation prepared correctly the first time, rather than resubmitting after a rejection</li>
</ul>
</div>
</details>
<details class="plan-chapter" id="project-detail-6"><summary><span class="chapter-count">06</span><h3>Water Conservation Support Available in Broomfield</h3>
<span aria-hidden="true" class="chapter-toggle">+</span></summary><div class="chapter-body prose"><p>Broomfield offers more than one path to support, including a residential partnership with Resource Central and a separate program for HOAs and larger properties converting turf. Program terms and funding change over time, so we won’t list dollar figures here. Our team can help point you toward what’s currently available during your estimate.</p>
</div>
</details>
<details class="plan-chapter" id="project-detail-7"><summary><span class="chapter-count">07</span><h3>Why Broomfield Homeowners and HOAs Choose Ridgewell</h3>
<span aria-hidden="true" class="chapter-toggle">+</span></summary><div class="chapter-body prose"><p>Founded in 2011 by longtime Colorado resident Francisco Garcia, Ridgewell Landscape and Design has spent more than a decade working within Colorado’s specific mix of climate demands and HOA expectations. We’re licensed and insured, hold a perfect 5.0-star rating on Google, and keep our team small enough that Francisco and Chris personally handle most projects and the communication that comes with them.</p>
</div>
</details>
<details class="plan-chapter" id="project-detail-8"><summary><span class="chapter-count">08</span><h3>Get Your Free HOA-Friendly Xeriscaping Estimate in Broomfield</h3>
<span aria-hidden="true" class="chapter-toggle">+</span></summary><div class="chapter-body prose"><p>If you want a design that satisfies both Colorado’s climate and your HOA’s covenants, let’s talk.<a href="/contact/"> Get Your Free Estimate</a> and our team will follow up.</p>

<hr/></div>
</details>
<details class="plan-chapter" id="project-detail-9"><summary><span class="chapter-count">09</span><h3>Your questions, answered</h3>
<span aria-hidden="true" class="chapter-toggle">+</span></summary><div class="chapter-body prose"><h3><strong>Can my Broomfield HOA reject my xeriscape design?</strong> </h3>

<p>Colorado state law now requires HOAs to allow at least a few pre-approved water-wise garden designs in front yards, so a reasonable xeriscape plan generally cannot be flatly refused. Individual HOA guidelines still vary in the details, so it helps to review your community’s specific covenants before submitting a design.</p>

<h3><strong>What does Broomfield’s Xeriscaping Guide cover?</strong> </h3>

<p>The city publishes specific guidance for landscaping the strip between the sidewalk and the street, an area many cities leave unaddressed. It outlines what’s expected in that narrow space, which is often the first thing an HOA architectural committee or city inspector notices.</p>

<h3><strong>What’s the difference between Broomfield’s residential and large-property programs?</strong> </h3>

<p>Broomfield partners with Resource Central to support individual residential lawn replacement, while a separate turf replacement rebate program targets HOAs, multi-family properties, and commercial customers converting larger areas of nonfunctional turf. The two serve different scales of project.</p>

<h3><strong>How long does HOA approval typically add to a project timeline?</strong> </h3>

<p>It varies by community, but building in a few extra weeks for architectural review is reasonable. Submitting a complete, well-documented design the first time tends to move faster than an incomplete one that gets sent back for revisions.</p>

<h3><strong>Can I xeriscape just my front yard to meet HOA visibility requirements?</strong> </h3>

<p>Yes. Many Broomfield homeowners start with the front yard, since that’s usually what HOA guidelines focus on most closely, and consider the backyard as a separate, less restricted project later.</p>

<h3><strong>Is xeriscaping allowed in newer Broomfield communities like Anthem?</strong> </h3>

<p>Generally yes, though specific plant lists and design guidelines can be more detailed in newer master-planned communities. Reviewing your HOA’s architectural guidelines before finalizing a design helps avoid surprises during approval.</p>
</div>
</details>
